DESCRIPTION:CBSE XII · Phase 1 · Day 5/9 · 2026-10-27\n1:00 PM–2:00 PM
  · 60 minutes · Asia/Kolkata (IST)\n\nTIME BUDGET\n5 min — Recall yest
 erday’s base\n30 min — Lesson and matching NCERT passage\n20 min — A
 ttempt the named examples / check items\n5 min — Check and record one co
 rrection\n\nWORK ORDER\nOpen the NCERT section named in step 1. Use a matc
 hing video explanation OR the worked example within the learning budget\; 
 do not try to finish both. Attempt the step 2 questions in the listed orde
 r with solutions covered. Reserve the checking minutes. At the practice cu
 toff\, flag any unattempted or unresolved question by number\; it goes to 
 the buffer or next same-subject slot.\n\n1. LEARN / RECALL\nNCERT XI 8.7.2
 –8.7.3\, PDF pp. 17–19. A nucleophile donates an electron pair\; an el
 ectrophile accepts it.\n\n2. INDEPENDENT PRACTICE\nWorked Problems 8.12 an
 d 8.13\; bridge check Q7\, Q8\, Q9 and Q12. Explain each classification in
  one sentence.\n\n3. CHECK & FINISH\nRecognise neutral nucleophiles as wel
 l as charged ones\; identify the electron-deficient carbon.\n\nVIDEO USE\n
 The linked window is a suggested continuation\, not a verified timestamp f
 or today’s topic. Check its heading first. Use it only if it explains th
 e NCERT topic named above\; if it does not\, use the named NCERT passage a
 nd worked example instead. Spend at most 2 minutes locating a matching exp
 lanation. Watching the whole window is optional\; stop at the end of the l
 earning budget and begin the questions.\n\nRESOURCES\nPDF page numbers cou
 nt from the first file page.\nMain lesson · 2:00:00–2:30:00\nhttps://ww
 w.youtube.com/watch?v=ZKIXgQOIZvc&t=7200s\n\nNCERT · today’s section / 
 questions\nhttps://ncert.nic.in/textbook/pdf/kech202.pdf#page=18\n\nOrgani
 c check · questions and answers\nSaved file: Resources/phase-01/organic-b
 ridge-check.html\nThe assigned bridge questions and answer checks are incl
 uded below.\n\nNCERT practice / worked model · PDF page 19\nhttps://ncert
 .nic.in/textbook/pdf/kech202.pdf#page=19\n\n\nORGANIC BRIDGE — original 
 prerequisite questions\, not CBSE PYQs\nQ7. Explain why both OH− and NH3
  can act as nucleophiles\, although NH3 is neutral.\nQ8. Why can BF3 and C
 H3+ act as electrophiles?\nQ9. Mark the partially positive and partially n
 egative ends of the C–Br bond in CH3Br. Which atom can a nucleophile att
 ack?\nQ12. In the protonation of NH3 by H+\, from where to where should th
 e curved electron-pair arrow point?\n\nANSWER CHECK — read only after at
 tempting the questions\nQ7. Each has an available lone pair that can be do
 nated to an electron-deficient centre. A nucleophile need not carry a nega
 tive charge.\nQ8. They have an electron-deficient centre with an incomplet
 e octet and can accept an electron pair.\nQ9. Carbon δ+–Br δ−. The n
 ucleophile attacks the carbon\; Br is more electronegative.\nQ12. From the
  nitrogen lone pair to H+. Arrows show electron movement\, not the directi
 on in which a positive ion travels.\n\nIF UNFINISHED\nReserve the checking
  time. Record the exact question or step and flag Needs repair in the HTML
  planner. Use the 11:45 PM buffer if needed\; stop at midnight. Remaining 
 work carries into the next same-subject block\, using explanation/replay t
 ime first.\n\nSAVED FILES & PROGRESS\nOpen planner.html from the project f
 older for saved PDFs\, reference cards and progress tracking. Saved files 
 are not uploaded or embedded in this calendar\; local file links do not re
 liably open from Google Calendar. Public links above work independently. C
 alendar events do not sync completion back to the HTML planner.
