 Why is phenol more acidic than ethanol? Think about the stability of the c
 onjugate bases.\nQ17. Why is methylamine more basic than ammonia in the st
 andard aqueous comparison? Do not extend this one comparison into a univer
 sal ranking of all amines.\n\nANSWER CHECK — read only after attempting 
 the questions\nQ14. CH3–C(=O)–O− ↔ CH3–C(–O−)=O. The two oxy
 gen atoms exchange the formal double-bond/charge roles\; atom positions do
  not change. The real ion is a resonance hybrid.\nQ16. Phenoxide distribut
 es negative charge through resonance\; ethoxide lacks comparable resonance
  delocalisation into an aromatic ring.\nQ17. The methyl group donates elec
 tron density through its +I effect\, increasing the availability of the ni
 trogen lone pair in this comparison. Solvation also matters when comparing
  different amines in water.\n\nIF UNFINISHED\nReserve the checking time. R
 ecord the exact question or step and flag Needs repair in the HTML planner
 . Use the 11:45 PM buffer if needed\; stop at midnight. Remaining work car
 ries into the next same-subject block\, using explanation/replay time firs
 t.\n\nSAVED FILES & PROGRESS\nOpen planner.html from the project folder fo
 r saved PDFs\, reference cards and progress tracking. Saved files are not 
 uploaded or embedded in this calendar\; local file links do not reliably o
 pen from Google Calendar. Public links above work independently. Calendar 
 events do not sync completion back to the HTML planner.
