 window is optional\; stop at the end of the learning budget and begin the 
 questions.\n\nRESOURCES\nPDF page numbers count from the first file page.\
 nMain lesson · 4:00:00–4:04:27\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZKIXgQO
 IZvc&t=14400s\n\nNCERT · today’s section / questions\nhttps://ncert.nic
 .in/textbook/pdf/kech202.pdf#page=18\n\nOrganic check · questions and ans
 wers\nSaved file: Resources/phase-01/organic-bridge-check.html\nThe assign
 ed bridge questions and answer checks are included below.\n\nNCERT practic
 e / worked model · PDF page 20\nhttps://ncert.nic.in/textbook/pdf/kech202
 .pdf#page=20\n\nNCERT practice / worked model · PDF page 21\nhttps://ncer
 t.nic.in/textbook/pdf/kech202.pdf#page=21\n\n\nORGANIC BRIDGE — original
  prerequisite questions\, not CBSE PYQs\nQ1. Name CH3CH2CH2Br using IUPAC 
 nomenclature.\nQ2. Draw the condensed structure of 2-methylpropan-1-ol.\nQ
 3. Identify the functional group in each: CH3CHO\, CH3COCH3\, CH3COOH\, CH
 3NH2\, CH3OCH3.\nQ4. Are CH3CH2OH and CH3OCH3 identical compounds or funct
 ional-group isomers? Explain using their molecular formula.\nQ5. State the
  hybridisation of each carbon atom in CH3CHO.\nQ6. Count sigma and pi bond
 s in ethene and ethyne.\nQ7. Explain why both OH− and NH3 can act as nuc
 leophiles\, although NH3 is neutral.\nQ8. Why can BF3 and CH3+ act as elec
 trophiles?\nQ9. Mark the partially positive and partially negative ends of
  the C–Br bond in CH3Br. Which atom can a nucleophile attack?\nQ10. Show
  the formal heterolytic cleavage of the C–Br bond in CH3Br\, assigning t
 he electron pair to Br. This is an electron-bookkeeping exercise\, not a c
 laim that a free methyl carbocation forms in an actual substitution.\nQ11.
  Show homolytic cleavage of Cl–Cl. How is the electron allocation differ
 ent from Q10?\nQ12. In the protonation of NH3 by H+\, from where to where 
 should the curved electron-pair arrow point?\nQ13. Arrange methyl\, primar
 y ethyl\, secondary isopropyl and tertiary tert-butyl carbocations in incr
 easing stability. Restrict the comparison to these simple alkyl carbocatio
 ns.\nQ14. Draw the two equivalent resonance contributors of CH3COO−. Do 
 any atom positions change?\nQ15. Which is the stronger acid\, CH3COOH or C
 lCH2COOH? Give the electronic reason.\nQ16. Why is phenol more acidic than
  ethanol? Think about the stability of the conjugate bases.\nQ17. Why is m
 ethylamine more basic than ammonia in the standard aqueous comparison? Do 
 not extend this one comparison into a universal ranking of all amines.\nQ1
 8. Classify each reaction as substitution\, elimination\, addition or oxid
 ation: (a) CH3Br + OH− → CH3OH + Br−\; (b) ethanol → ethene + wate
 r on acid-catalysed heating\; (c) ethene + HBr → bromoethane\; (d) ethan
 ol → ethanal using an oxidising agent.\nQ19. If one substituent is at ca
 rbon 1 of benzene\, which positions are ortho\, meta and para to it?\nQ20.
  For the electron-pair movement in CH3Br + OH− → CH3OH + Br−\, draw 
 an arrow from an oxygen lone pair to carbon and another from the C–Br bo
 nd to bromine. Explain what each arrow means. Full SN1/SN2 kinetics and st
 ereochemistry belong to the next chapter.\n\nANSWER CHECK — read only af
 ter attempting the questions\nQ1. 1-Bromopropane.\nQ2. (CH3)2CHCH2OH. The 
 –OH carbon must be carbon 1.\nQ3. Aldehyde\; ketone\; carboxylic acid\; 
 amine\; ether\, respectively.\nQ4. Functional-group isomers: both have for
 mula C2H6O\, with different connectivity and functional groups.\nQ5. The m
 ethyl carbon is sp3\; the carbonyl carbon is sp2.\nQ6. Ethene: 5 sigma and
  1 pi. Ethyne: 3 sigma and 2 pi.\nQ7. Each has an available lone pair that
  can be donated to an electron-deficient centre. A nucleophile need not ca
 rry a negative charge.\nQ8. They have an electron-deficient centre with an
  incomplete octet and can accept an electron pair.\nQ9. Carbon δ+–Br δ
 −. The nucleophile attacks the carbon\; Br is more electronegative.\nQ10
 . CH3Br → CH3+ + Br−\, with both bonding electrons allocated to Br. Us
 e a full curved arrow from the bond to Br.\nQ11. Cl–Cl → Cl· + ·Cl. 
 Each atom receives one bonding electron\; show fishhook arrows if drawing 
 the movement.\nQ12. From the nitrogen lone pair to H+. Arrows show electro
 n movement\, not the direction in which a positive ion travels.\nQ13. CH3+
  < CH3CH2+ < (CH3)2CH+ < (CH3)3C+. Alkyl groups stabilise positive charge 
 through electron donation and hyperconjugation. Resonance-stabilised ions 
 require a separate comparison.\nQ14. CH3–C(=O)–O− ↔ CH3–C(–O
 −)=O. The two oxygen atoms exchange the formal double-bond/charge roles\
 ; atom positions do not change. The real ion is a resonance hybrid.\nQ15. 
 ClCH2COOH. Chlorine withdraws electron density by its −I effect and stab
 ilises the conjugate base.\nQ16. Phenoxide distributes negative charge thr
 ough resonance\; ethoxide lacks comparable resonance delocalisation into a
 n aromatic ring.\nQ17. The methyl group donates electron density through i
 ts +I effect\, increasing the availability of the nitrogen lone pair in th
 is comparison. Solvation also matters when comparing different amines in w
 ater.\nQ18. (a) Substitution\; (b) elimination\; (c) addition\; (d) oxidat
 ion.\nQ19. Ortho: 2 and 6. Meta: 3 and 5. Para: 4. Recognition only\; chap
 ter-specific directing rules come later.\nQ20. The first arrow shows oxyge
 n donating an electron pair to form a C–O bond. The second shows the C
 –Br electron pair going to Br\, producing Br−. Carbon must not be draw
 n with five completed covalent bonds in the product.\n\nIF UNFINISHED\nRes
 erve the checking time. Record the exact question or step and flag Needs r
 epair in the HTML planner. Use the 11:45 PM buffer if needed\; stop at mid
 night. Remaining work carries into the next same-subject block\, using exp
 lanation/replay time first.\n\nSAVED FILES & PROGRESS\nOpen planner.html f
 rom the project folder for saved PDFs\, reference cards and progress track
 ing. Saved files are not uploaded or embedded in this calendar\; local fil
 e links do not reliably open from Google Calendar. Public links above work
  independently. Calendar events do not sync completion back to the HTML pl
 anner.
